.TH dump "" "Administration"
.SH NAME
dump \- file system dump
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBdump [\fIoptions\fB] [\fIargument ...\fB] \fIfilesystem\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
dump -- file system dump
Usage:	dump [ options ] [ argument ...  ]
Options:
	0-9	Set dump level (default: 9u)
	b 	Next argument is blocking factor (default: 20)
	d	Next argument is density in bpi (default: 1600)
	f	Next argument is output device name
	s 	Next argument is tape length in feet (default: 2300)
	S	Next argument is floppy disk size in blocks
	u	Update /etc/ddate
	v	Verbose (display date and tape length)
.HE
.B dump
copies to tape (or to a disk file) every file in
.I filesystem
that was created or changed since some earlier dump of that file
system, together with the full directory structure needed to find
those files again.
.PP
The first argument, if any, is a single word made up of the letters
.BR 0123456789bdfsSuv ,
with no leading
.BR \- ,
giving the dump level and processing options; any letter among
.B bdfsS
consumes the following argument as its value.
After those, the last remaining argument, if any, names the
.I filesystem
(a block-special file or the mounted directory) to dump; without a
.I filesystem
argument
.B dump
refuses to run.
.IP \fB0\-9\fR 0.5i
Set the dump level.
A level-0 dump backs up every file.
A dump at level
.I n
(the default is level 9 if no digit is given) backs up every file
changed since the most recent dump recorded in
.B /etc/ddate
at a level less than
.IR n .
.IP \fBb\fR
The next argument gives the blocking factor -- the number of dump
records per tape block -- as a number from 1 to 20 (default 20).
.IP \fBd\fR
The next argument gives the tape density in bytes per inch, from 800
to 6250 (default 1600); used only to estimate how much tape a dump
will use.
.IP \fBf\fR
The next argument gives the path name of the output device or file
(default
.BR /dev/dump ).
.IP \fBs\fR
The next argument gives the length of the output tape in feet, from
600 to 4800 (default 2300).
.B dump
tracks how much tape it has used and asks for a new volume before
running off the end.
.IP \fBS\fR
The next argument gives the size of the output device in 512-byte
blocks (for a floppy disk or a device with a fixed capacity, in place
of
.BR s ).
.IP \fBu\fR
After a successful dump, record its date and level in
.B /etc/ddate
so that a later incremental dump knows where to start.
.IP \fBv\fR
Print, on the standard error, the date the dump is measured from and,
at the end, the number of inodes, real blocks, and sparse blocks
dumped, and the length of tape (or blocks) used.
.PP
If more than one reel or floppy is needed, or if
.B S
was given,
.B dump
closes the current volume, prompts on the standard error to mount the
next one, and waits for a line to be typed before continuing; reaching
end of file at that prompt aborts the dump.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 1.6i
\fB/etc/ddate\fR	dump-date file, one entry per file system and level
\fB/dev/dump\fR	default output device
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B mkfs
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Most error conditions (a missing or unreadable file system, a write
error on the output device, or running out of memory) are fatal and
end the dump with exit status 1; a normal completion exits with
status 0.
.SH NOTES
The
.B u
option is enabled by default only when
.B dump
is invoked with no arguments at all -- and, since a
.I filesystem
argument is then also missing, that invocation always fails.
In practice, give
.B u
explicitly (as part of the option letters) whenever
.B /etc/ddate
should be updated.
